    <h1>
        What Does Vorticity Actually Measure?
    </h1>


    <p class="subtitle">

        A mathematical and physical interpretation of
        $\boldsymbol{\omega} = \nabla \times \mathbf{u}$.

    </p>


    <p>

        Vorticity is one of the most important quantities
        in fluid mechanics. It is also one of the most
        misunderstood.

    </p>


    <h2>
        1. Start with the velocity field
    </h2>


    <p>

        Consider a two-dimensional velocity field

        $$

        \mathbf{u}(x,y)
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        u(x,y) \\
        v(x,y)
        \end{bmatrix}.
        $$

        The velocity tells us how a fluid particle moves,
        but it does not immediately tell us how a small
        fluid element rotates.

    </p>


    <h2>
        2. Definition of vorticity
    </h2>


    <p>

        Vorticity is defined as the curl of the velocity:

    </p>


    <div class="equation">

        $$

        \boldsymbol{\omega}
        =
        \nabla \times \mathbf{u}

        $$

    </div>


    <p>

        For a two-dimensional flow,

    </p>


    <div class="equation">

        $$

        \omega_z
        =
        \frac{\partial v}{\partial x}
        -
        \frac{\partial u}{\partial y}

        $$

    </div>


    <h2>
        3. But what does it mean physically?
    </h2>


    <p>

        Imagine placing a tiny paddle wheel inside the flow.
        The local velocity gradients can cause the wheel to rotate.

        Vorticity is related to the local angular rotation
        of a fluid element.

    </p>


    <h2>
        4. A simple example
    </h2>


    <p>

        Consider solid-body rotation:

    </p>


    <div class="equation">

        $$

        u=-\Omega y,
        \qquad
        v=\Omega x.

        $$

    </div>


    <p>

        Therefore,

    </p>


    <div class="equation">

        $$

        \omega_z
        =
        \frac{\partial v}{\partial x}
        -
        \frac{\partial u}{\partial y}

        =
        \Omega - (-\Omega)

        =
        2\Omega.

        $$

    </div>


    <h2>
        5. The important distinction
    </h2>


    <p>

        Vorticity is not simply "how curved the flow looks."

        It is a local differential quantity obtained from
        velocity gradients.

    </p>


